This doc covers everything you need to plug your AI voice bot into our VaniSetu —
connecting, authenticating, and exchanging audio + control messages for live calls. It's
written for whoever's implementing the bot side, so we've kept it to what you actually need
rather than how things work under the hood.
A couple of notes before you start:
• We're happy to walk through this live if that's faster — just ping us.
• If something here doesn't match what you're actually seeing on the wire, tell us. This
doc gets updated from real integration issues, not written once and forgotten.
• Sample values (IDs, tokens, numbers) below are placeholders — swap in your real
credentials once provisioned.
1. Connection
VaniSetu URL = "wss://voice.varnet.in/v1/ai/connect"
Open one persistent WebSocket connection to this URL and keep it open — this single
connection carries every call on your account at once, multiplexed together. You don't open a
new connection per call, and you shouldn't try to; the multiplexing is intentional and is what
lets your bot handle concurrent calls without managing a socket per conversation.
Each call is identified by a numeric Session ID, assigned by VaniSetu the moment a call
becomes active. You'll receive this ID in an event (see below) and use it to tag every audio
frame and message related to that call — think of it as the "which conversation is this" tag on
everything that flows over the shared pipe.
If the connection drops, reconnect and re-authenticate — any calls already in progress on our
side will simply resume sending events once your connection is back up, so a brief reconnect
shouldn't lose call state on our end. We'd still recommend logging reconnects on your side so
you can see if it's happening more than expected.
2. Authentication
Immediately after connecting, send this as your first message (TEXT frame):
type WSAuthMessage struct {
 Type string `json:"type"`
 TCode string `json:"tcode"`
 Token string `json:"token"`
}
{
 "type": "auth",
 "tcode": "varnet",
 "token": "Bearer [from my .env of eva]"
}
tcode and token will be issued to you separately per account. On success you'll receive a
confirmation message; on failure, an error message followed by connection close.
One thing that trips people up on first integration: this has to be the very first message on the
socket, before anything else — including before you'd normally send any kind of "ready"
ping from your side. If auth doesn't arrive first, we close the connection.
3. Audio — Binary Frames
All audio, in both directions, travels as BINARY WebSocket frames. Every frame carries a
4-byte Session ID prefix identifying which call the audio belongs to, followed by the raw
audio bytes:
[ 4 bytes: Session ID (big-endian uint32) ][ audio bytes ]
Receiving audio from a caller: read the first 4 bytes as the Session ID, everything after that
is audio.
Sending audio back to a caller: prepend the same Session ID (the one this conversation
belongs to) as the first 4 bytes, then your audio bytes.
The Session ID must only ever be attached as a raw 4-byte prefix on BINARY frames —
never on TEXT/JSON messages (see below). We've had a couple of integrations accidentally
do this the other way early on, so it's worth double-checking your framing code against the
diagram above before your first real test call.
4. Events & Commands — Text Frames (JSON)
All non-audio messages are TEXT frames, wrapped in a simple envelope carrying the
Session ID as a proper JSON field:
{
 "session_id": 123456,
 "payload": { "...event or command body..." }
}
• Messages we send you are called events.
• Messages you send us are called commands.
• session_id identifies which call the message concerns. It is the same numeric ID
used in the binary audio prefix for that call.
• Some messages (see INCOMING_CALL below) are not yet tied to a session and are sent
without the envelope, as-is.
5. Events (VaniSetu → You)
INCOMING_CALL
Sent the moment a new call arrives, before any Session ID exists. Sent as-is — not wrapped
in the envelope.
{
 "event": "INCOMING_CALL",
 "call_id": "1787400283.184"
}
You may store call_id for your own reference; it is not required in any request today, but
keeping it is recommended for future use.
MEDIA_START
Sent once a call's media path becomes active. Wrapped in the standard envelope.
{
 "session_id": 123456,
 "payload": {
 "event": "MEDIA_START",
 "channel_id": "1787403732.230",
 "format": "ulaw",
 "optimal_frame_size": 160,
 "ptime": 20
 }
}
Field Meaning
channel_id Internal call reference (informational)
format Audio codec in use for this call
optimal_frame_size Recommended audio chunk size, in bytes, for this call
ptime Packetization interval in milliseconds
Use format/optimal_frame_size/ptime to size your outbound audio chunks correctly for
this specific call — they can vary call to call, so please don't hardcode these on your end even
if the first few test calls all come back the same.
6. Commands (You → VaniSetu)
All commands below are session-scoped and must be sent inside the envelope, e.g.:
{
 "session_id": 123456,
 "payload": { "command": "ANSWER" }
}
Command Purpose When to use
ANSWER Answer the call Before any audio/response is sent to
the caller
HANGUP End the call When the conversation is complete
FLUSH_MEDIA Immediately stop/discard audio
queued for playback
Caller starts speaking while you're
still playing a response (barge-in)
PAUSE_MEDIA Temporarily pause playback Hold current playback without
discarding it
CONTINUE_MEDIA Resume playback after a pause After PAUSE_MEDIA
START_MEDIA_BUFFERING Signal you're about to send an
audio response in multiple chunks Before streaming a multi-chunk reply
GET_STATUS Request current call status Diagnostics / health checks
REPORT_QUEUE_DRAINED Request notification once all sent
audio has finished playing
Knowing when it's safe to end a turn
cleanly
Sample commands:
{"session_id": 123456, "payload": {"command": "HANGUP"}}
{"session_id": 123456, "payload": {"command": "FLUSH_MEDIA"}}
{"session_id": 123456, "payload": {"command": "PAUSE_MEDIA"}}
{"session_id": 123456, "payload": {"command": "GET_STATUS"}}
OUTBOUND_CALL — placing a call (sessionless)
Used when you want to initiate a call rather than respond to an incoming one. Since no call
exists yet, there is no Session ID — this command is sent without session_id:
{
 "payload": {
 "command": "OUTBOUND_CALL",
 "endpoint": "07415636404",
 "caller_id": "+917314648232",
 "request_id": "req-1787392315001786200"
 }
}
Field Meaning
endpoint Number to call
caller_id Caller ID to present
request_id A unique string you generate, used to match this request with its result
Once the call connects, you'll receive events for it (including its assigned Session ID) so
subsequent commands/audio can reference it normally.
A note from our side: outbound calling is newer than the rest of this integration, so if the
timing between sending OUTBOUND_CALL and receiving the follow-up event feels slow or
inconsistent during testing, let us know what you're seeing — we're still tuning this path.
